WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Thanks for tuning in to First Principles Daily Episode 12 for June 17th, 2026.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.640
Today, an industry whose idiot index is begging to be attacked,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.220
rockets once priced at roughly $10,000 per kilogram to orbit

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.740
now reach a few thousand through reuse,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.180
because the question changed from building expendables cheaper

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.020
to whether the hardware must be thrown away after one flight.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.120
For most of the space age, a Falcon 9 class booster

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.480
would have been discarded after a single flight.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:35.060
Its tanks, engines, and avionics would have been committed to the ocean

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.620
with no thought of recovery.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.820
Today, the same booster returns lands and flies again,

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.220
sometimes more than a dozen times.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.120
That change turns the dominant cost item from fabrication

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.340
into inspection and refurbishment.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.700
The shift did not come from asking how to make each new rocket slightly less expensive.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.260
It came from asking what physics actually demands

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.220
must be replaced after every launch and what can be retained.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:07.560
Launch providers inherited a pattern set by the first ICBM-derived vehicles.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.980
The rocket existed only to deliver its payload.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.960
After that, the structure had served its purpose and was abandoned.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.400
That convention spread because each national program copied the approach

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.580
that had already worked.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.240
Then, they optimized within it by shaving a few percent off tank fabrication

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.600
or engine assembly.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.840
The result was a mature industry in which every kilogram delivered to low-earth orbit

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.380
carried an amortized cost on the order of $10,000 or more.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:41.940
That cost was driven almost entirely by the fact that the vehicle itself was written off

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.020
after one use.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.760
No one inside the established firms treated the expendability as a variable.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.420
It was simply the way rockets were built.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:56.300
Consequently, the cost per kilogram moved only when governments funded larger vehicles

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.260
or accepted lower flight rates.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.840
It never moved because the hardware itself was asked to do more work.

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:08.020
The underlying assumption remained that the energy and materials invested in a booster

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.880
were single use by nature.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.000
An assumption no one revisited because the analogy of a missile that flies once

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.840
had become the entire frame.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.920
SpaceX began by separating the requirement of reaching orbit from the requirement of building

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.820
a new rocket for every mission.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:30.880
The physics floor is set by the energy needed to accelerate roughly 20 tons of payload.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.640
It is also set by the structural mass needed to contain several hundred tons of propellant.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.020
That energy and that mass do not inherently disappear after one flight.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:47.220
A rough magic wand estimate for the materials in a Falcon 9 first stage

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.000
sits in the low hundreds of thousands of dollars at commodity prices.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.580
Those materials include aluminum lithium tanks,

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.040
merlin engines, avionics, wiring, and thermal protection.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:06.560
By contrast, the completed stage under the old expendable model carried an effective cost

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:07.860
measured in the millions.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:14.500
That ratio already pointed to an idiot index well above 10 before reuse was even attempted.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:17.840
To see why, compare the two figures directly.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:23.700
If the raw materials cost a few hundred thousand dollars and the finished expendable stage cost

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.680
several million, the multiplier lands comfortably above 10.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:34.180
That gap signals that design and process choices, not the atoms themselves, are driving most of

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.860
price. The first concrete move was therefore to retain the stage rather than discard it.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:45.100
Retention required proving that the tanks and engines could survive re-entry heating,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.320
aerodynamic loads, and landing impact.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:54.160
One survival was shown attention turned to lowering the cost of returning the stage to flight

00:03:54.160 --> 00:04:00.120
condition. propellant manifolds were redesigned so fewer separate lines and joints were needed.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.320
That change removed potential leak paths and inspection hours.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.300
Grid fins and landing legs were added as reusable elements.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.780
Their mass penalty was accepted because it eliminated the need to build an entirely new

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:16.840
attitude control and recovery system for each flight.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:23.360
Engine restart sequences were simplified by changing valve timing logic rather than adding hardware.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.480
That cut both part count and the number of pre-flight checks.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.920
Over successive versions the turbo pump assemblies moved from multiple machine forging to

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.560
configurations that combined several functions into single cast or additively manufactured housings.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:46.760
Each consolidation removed mass, seals, and assembly labor because each of these steps attacked

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:52.640
the refurbishment cost directly. The marginal expense of a second flight fell far below the cost of

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.880
building a fresh stage. That movement lowered the effective idiot index even though the raw material

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:05.660
floor itself had not changed. Falcon 9 flights now achieved costs per kilogram to low-Earth orbit

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:10.600
reported in the low thousands of dollars when averaged across many reuses.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:17.140
That marks a clear reduction from the prior industry baseline. Yet the figure remains well above

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:23.380
the theoretical material floor. The current refurbishment process keeps the idiot index of a

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.640
reused booster perhaps three to five times the magic wand materials estimate.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.320
That stands in contrast to the double digit figure of a purely expendable design.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.400
Limits remain visible because the engines still require post-flight work that cannot yet be

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:46.420
eliminated by design alone. The fairing recovery system, while demonstrated, adds its own

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:52.000
inspection overhead. The approach also trades some payload performance for the propellant margin

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.920
needed to return the booster. That explicit compromise was accepted because the reduction in

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:04.560
amortized hardware cost outweighs the lost mass. Further gains will require either dramatically

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:11.080
cheaper inspection methods or a booster architecture whose engines and tanks need essentially no

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:17.080
refurbishment between flights. A vehicle whose price greatly exceeds its contained materials

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:23.060
is signaling that the design or the operational concept not the physics is carrying the cost.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:29.360
The signal appears whenever the finished item costs many times more than the raw materials inside

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:35.980
it. In the case of an expendable booster the multiplier set well above 10 because the entire stage

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:42.320
was treated as single use. Questioning whether the entire rocket must be replaced after one mission

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:47.280
opened a lever that incremental improvements inside the expendable frame could never reach.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.200
The separation began with a simple distinction. Reaching orbit is a physics requirement.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:59.320
Building a new rocket for every mission is an inherited assumption. Once those two ideas were

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:04.260
pulled apart every subsequent redesign could target the refurbishment cost instead of the

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:09.940
fabrication cost. The same pattern of separating physics requirements from inherited assumptions

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:16.340
now sits ready for any hardware that is built once and discarded. Consider a pressure vessel

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:22.760
an aircraft fuselage section or a high value industrial pump. Each carries an idiot index

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:28.300
set by how many times its materials are asked to perform before they are thrown away.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:35.180
When that index stays high the opportunity lies not in cheaper raw stock but in rethinking whether

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:41.820
replacement is required at all. The Falcon 9 example shows that proving survival and then simplifying

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:48.160
return to flight steps can compress the index dramatically. The same sequence can be applied wherever

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:54.140
a product is currently designed around a single cycle of use. What would change first if the next

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:59.220
generation of launch stages were designed from the outset around repeated flight rather than

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:05.900
around a single use? That's First Principles Daily for today. If this changed how you see what

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:11.520
things cost share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:17.860
in Vancouver. One example or one opportunity every day. See you tomorrow and if you'd rather watch

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:25.720
than listen find us on YouTube at Nerra Network. Links in the show notes. This episode used AI voice

